The '''2012–13 Saudi Professional League''' (known as the '''[[Zain Group|Zain]] Professional League''' for sponsorship reasons) was the 37th season of the [[Saudi Pro League]], the top Saudi professional league for [[association football]] clubs, since its establishment in [[1976–77 Saudi Premier League|1976]]. The season began on 2 August 2012 and ended on 27 April 2013.<ref>{{cite web |title=جدول الدوري |url=http://www.slstat.com/spl2012-2013ar/champtable.php |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160421060010/http://www.slstat.com/spl2012-2013ar/champtable.php |archive-date=21 April 2016 |url-status=dead}}</ref> [[Al Shabab FC (Riyadh)|Al-Shabab]] were the [[2011–12 Saudi Pro League|defending champions]].<ref>{{cite web |title=الشباب بطلاً لدوري زين |url=https://www.aleqt.com/2012/04/14/article_646956.html}}</ref> The league was contested by the 12 teams from the [[2011–12 Saudi Pro League|2011–12 season]] as well as [[Al-Shoulla FC|Al-Shoulla]] and [[Al Wehda FC|Al-Wehda]], who joined as the promoted clubs from the [[2011–12 Saudi First Division|2011–12 First Division]]. They replace [[Al-Ansar FC (Medina)|Al-Ansar]] and [[Al Qadsiah FC|Al-Qadsiah]] who were relegated to the [[2012–13 Saudi First Division|2012–13 First Division]].

On 14 April, [[Al Fateh SC|Al-Fateh]] won their first league title, with two games to spare, following a 1–0 home win over [[Al-Ahli Saudi FC|Al-Ahli]].<ref>{{cite web |title=الفتح يحقق لقب الدوري للمرة الأولى في تاريخه |url=https://www.alriyadh.com/826251}}</ref> Al-Fateh became the seventh club to win the Pro League. This is considered by many to be one of the greatest shocks in Saudi football history, especially considering that Al-Fateh were promoted to the Pro League for the first time only four years earlier.<ref>{{cite web |title=حقق الدرع للمرة الأولى بعد 55 عاماً على تأسيس النادي |url=https://www.albayan.ae/sports/arab/2013-04-16-1.1863129}}</ref>

[[Al Wehda FC|Al-Wehda]] were the first team to be relegated following a 2–1 home defeat to [[Al Faisaly FC|Al-Faisaly]] on 29 March.<ref>{{cite web |title=نقطتين تفصل الفتح عن (زين).. الهلال يتشبث بالأمل من شباك الاتحاد.. والفيصلي يكتب قصيدة وداع الوحدة |url=http://slstat.com/spl2012-2013ar/news.php?id=758 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160507125927/http://slstat.com/spl2012-2013ar/news.php?id=758 |archive-date=7 May 2016}}</ref> [[Hajer FC|Hajer]] became the second and final team to be relegated following a 2–1 defeat away to [[Al Shabab FC (Riyadh)|Al-Shabab]] on the final matchday.<ref>{{cite web |title=الشباب يوقع على هبوط هجر.. والحياة تعود إلى عروق التعاون ب البقاء |url=https://www.alriyadh.com/830115}}</ref>

==Teams==
Fourteen teams competed in the league&nbsp;– the twelve teams from the previous season and the two teams promoted from the [[2011–12 Saudi First Division|First Division]]. The promoted teams were [[Al-Shoulla FC|Al-Shoulla]] (returning after an absence of eight seasons) and [[Al Wehda FC|Al-Wehda]] (returning after a season's absence). They replaced [[Al Qadsiah FC|Al-Qadsiah]] (ending their three-year top-flight spell) and [[Al-Ansar FC (Medina)|Al-Ansar]] (who were relegated after one season in the top flight).

